  "text": "This short paper continues the description of the grammar used in the first version of a documentary indexing system that was previously presented in a scholarly journal. It first reviews how meaningful content units can be linked together, then outlines the main rules that guide the assembly of those units. Understanding these rules helps the information specialist lower the effort needed to acquire and operate the system. The meaningful units are also broken down into smaller, relevant fragments that can be used for indexing. The next section lists the syntactic rules of the language, followed by a discussion of its properties: the benefits and drawbacks of using a natural‑language‑like approach, the problems that arise when an indexing language has a fixed syntax (such as synonym handling, user difficulty, and processing cost), and the ways the proposed language addresses these issues while remaining flexible. Two annexes finish the article, one summarizing the language’s features and the other providing sample indexing entries."
